  "preamble": "Loss of retroflexion in the nasal: [ɳ] becomes dental-alveolar [n]. In the history of Hindi, Middle Indo-Aryan (Prakrit and Apabhramsa) ṇ and ṇṇ appear as n in inherited words (pāṇiu > pānī 'water', kaṇṇa > kān 'ear'). Bengali and Assamese have lost /ɳ/ as a phoneme altogether: ⟨ণ⟩ is pronounced [n] in inherited words and Sanskrit loanwords alike, except in clusters with retroflex stops. In casual Hindi and in Urdu, Sanskrit loanword /ɳ/ is usually replaced by /n/. Punjabi, Gujarati, Odia and Marathi keep singleton /ɳ/.",
  "phoneticEffects": "Deretroflexion, Merger",
  "languageExamples": [
    {
      "language": "Hindi",
      "languageFamily": "Central Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Sauraseni Prakrit kaṇṇa (< Sanskrit karṇa)",
          "to": "कान /kɑːn/ 'ear'",
          "note": "Prakrit ṇṇ > Hindi n"
        },
        {
          "from": "Sauraseni Prakrit soṇṇa (< Sanskrit sauvarṇa)",
          "to": "सोना /soː.nɑː/ 'gold'",
          "note": "Same development"
        },
        {
          "from": "Apabhramsa pāṇiu (< Prakrit pāṇīa < Sanskrit pānīya)",
          "to": "पानी /pɑː.niː/ 'water'",
          "note": "Singleton ṇ > n; Old Hindi pā̃nī"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "language": "Bengali",
      "languageFamily": "Eastern Indo-Aryan",
      "examples": [
        {
          "from": "Prakrit kaṇṇa (< Sanskrit karṇa)",
          "to": "কান /kan/ 'ear'",
          "note": "Inherited word"
        },
        {
          "from": "Magadhi Prakrit soaṇṇa/soṇṇa (< Sanskrit sauvarṇa)",
          "to": "সোনা /ʃona/ 'gold'",
          "note": "Inherited word"
        },
        {
          "from": "Sanskrit गुण guṇa",
          "to": "গুণ /ɡun/ 'quality, virtue'",
          "note": "Learned loanword; ⟨ণ⟩ is pronounced [n] outside clusters with retroflex stops"
